WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:01.870
1929.

00:00:01.870 --> 00:00:04.120
Hollywood Roosevelt Hotel.

00:00:04.120 --> 00:00:08.230
This is the site of the
first-ever Academy Awards,

00:00:08.230 --> 00:00:11.174
or as it's more commonly
referred to, the Oscars.

00:00:12.270 --> 00:00:14.870
Known for its glitz and glam, the Oscars

00:00:14.870 --> 00:00:18.860
also represent the best of
the best in the film industry,

00:00:18.860 --> 00:00:23.000
which makes it even more
tragic to watch it die.

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:25.720
The 2018 Oscars drew record-low ratings,

00:00:25.720 --> 00:00:29.380
dropping nearly 20%
from the previous year,

00:00:29.380 --> 00:00:32.300
and even if we disregard
the inevitable snubs

00:00:32.300 --> 00:00:35.050
and drama surrounding the host every year,

00:00:35.050 --> 00:00:39.130
it's evident that the
Oscars have a problem.

00:00:39.130 --> 00:00:39.963
They're rigged.

00:00:46.070 --> 00:00:49.460
So why are the Oscars such a big deal?

00:00:49.460 --> 00:00:51.590
A recent study found that studios,

00:00:51.590 --> 00:00:54.900
on average, spend an extra $10 million

00:00:54.900 --> 00:00:57.660
just to run an Oscar campaign.

00:00:57.660 --> 00:01:00.240
Now, it may sound like a lot, but it's

00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:02.430
actually not a bad investment.

00:01:02.430 --> 00:01:03.730
Over the past four years,

00:01:03.730 --> 00:01:07.320
best picture winners generated
an additional $19 million

00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:08.860
at the box office.

00:01:08.860 --> 00:01:13.070
That's more than 42%
of total ticket sales.

00:01:13.070 --> 00:01:14.460
"The King's Speech", for example,

00:01:14.460 --> 00:01:16.270
was initially projected to gross

00:01:16.270 --> 00:01:20.430
just $30 million, but after
its subsequent nomination

00:01:20.430 --> 00:01:22.400
and victory, it went on to make

00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:26.370
more than $400 million in the box office.

00:01:26.370 --> 00:01:29.650
Today, the Oscars aren't
just an awards ceremony.

00:01:29.650 --> 00:01:32.150
They're also a marketing event,

00:01:32.150 --> 00:01:34.770
but it wasn't always like this.

00:01:34.770 --> 00:01:36.430
"The Deer Hunter", a best picture

00:01:36.430 --> 00:01:39.260
from 1978, was the first film ever

00:01:39.260 --> 00:01:42.780
to use the Oscars as a marketing strategy.

00:01:42.780 --> 00:01:45.000
Universal Studios
worried the film would be

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:49.270
a commercial failure due to
its grim and depressing tone

00:01:49.270 --> 00:01:51.410
and came to a realization that the

00:01:51.410 --> 00:01:56.410
only way to draw a crowd
was by winning the Oscar.

00:01:56.580 --> 00:01:57.413
And it worked.

00:01:58.540 --> 00:02:00.300
Another figure who took advantage

00:02:00.300 --> 00:02:03.900
of the system was none
other than Harvey Weinstein.

00:02:03.900 --> 00:02:06.850
The controversial film
producer was notorious

00:02:06.850 --> 00:02:11.070
for his aggressive marketing
strategies to win best picture.

00:02:11.070 --> 00:02:14.100
It's how "Shakespeare in
Love" took home the prize

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:18.650
in 1999 over Spielberg's
"Saving Private Ryan",

00:02:18.650 --> 00:02:21.320
an obvious favorite at the time.

00:02:21.320 --> 00:02:23.760
It's no surprise that Hollywood pours

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:27.510
so much attention and
money into the event,

00:02:27.510 --> 00:02:30.270
but there is another secret.

00:02:30.270 --> 00:02:33.630
The Oscars aren't that difficult to win.

00:02:33.630 --> 00:02:36.810
That is, as long as you
understand the rules of the game,

00:02:36.810 --> 00:02:39.910
and the key is all in the statistics.

00:02:39.910 --> 00:02:42.920
For instance, you wanna make longer films,

00:02:42.920 --> 00:02:45.530
ideally over two hours.

00:02:45.530 --> 00:02:47.360
This graph charts the runtime of

00:02:47.360 --> 00:02:50.270
all best picture winners since 1929.

00:02:50.270 --> 00:02:54.090
There are no distinct patterns,
but in terms of probability,

00:02:54.090 --> 00:02:57.300
longer films have a
better chance of winning.

00:02:57.300 --> 00:02:59.980
Only three out of 90 winners had a runtime

00:02:59.980 --> 00:03:04.980
below 100 minutes, while
28 ran below two hours.

00:03:05.450 --> 00:03:10.330
And 76% of all winners
since 1960 have been

00:03:10.330 --> 00:03:12.850
more than two hours long.

00:03:12.850 --> 00:03:16.560
So why do the Oscars love longer films?

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:18.030
The answer is simple:

00:03:18.030 --> 00:03:21.620
Because longer films tend
to feel more important,

00:03:21.620 --> 00:03:23.540
and if there's one thing we can agree on,

00:03:23.540 --> 00:03:28.220
the Oscars love important
movies, almost to a fault.

00:03:28.220 --> 00:03:29.470
These are not important movies

00:03:29.470 --> 00:03:32.000
in the context of cinematic achievements

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:35.510
but rather the buzz that surrounds a film.

00:03:35.510 --> 00:03:38.400
It's the same reason why
film marketers work hard

00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:41.530
to create that Oscar buzz, like the extent

00:03:41.530 --> 00:03:43.240
to which actors went to prepare

00:03:43.240 --> 00:03:46.740
for a role or the difficulties
behind the scenes.

00:03:46.740 --> 00:03:49.610
There's no reason why
runtime should have an effect

00:03:49.610 --> 00:03:52.730
on whether a film wins
the best picture or not,

00:03:52.730 --> 00:03:55.360
yet, it clearly does.

00:03:55.360 --> 00:03:58.470
This obsession with important
films is the same reason

00:03:58.470 --> 00:04:01.670
why dramas are the king
of the award season.

00:04:01.670 --> 00:04:06.610
A whopping 93% of best
picture winners are dramas,

00:04:06.610 --> 00:04:10.220
while only 2% are action and fantasy.

00:04:10.220 --> 00:04:13.500
This pattern extends beyond
the best picture category.

00:04:13.500 --> 00:04:16.410
A study found that actors were nine times

00:04:16.410 --> 00:04:18.620
more likely to receive a nomination

00:04:18.620 --> 00:04:21.320
for their work in a drama.

00:04:21.320 --> 00:04:25.140
Over its 90 years of
history, science fiction

00:04:25.140 --> 00:04:29.040
and horror movies have
never won best picture.

00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:31.150
It's why monumental works of cinema,

00:04:31.150 --> 00:04:34.410
like Stanley Kubrick's
"2001: A Space Odyssey"

00:04:34.410 --> 00:04:36.810
or Alfred Hitchcock's "Psycho" were

00:04:36.810 --> 00:04:39.060
never even nominated, and this is

00:04:39.060 --> 00:04:41.680
where we start to see the problem.

00:04:41.680 --> 00:04:45.420
There is a formula to winning the Oscars,

00:04:45.420 --> 00:04:46.810
and it's a formula that should

00:04:46.810 --> 00:04:49.890
not exist in the first place, especially

00:04:49.890 --> 00:04:53.600
in a ceremony dedicated
to rewarding good art.

00:04:53.600 --> 00:04:55.730
The concept of picking the best film

00:04:55.730 --> 00:04:59.110
to come out in any given year is ludicrous

00:04:59.110 --> 00:05:01.930
considering how subjective movies are.

00:05:01.930 --> 00:05:04.870
The Oscars are well aware of this,

00:05:04.870 --> 00:05:08.070
and instead, they choose
a different approach,

00:05:08.070 --> 00:05:11.870
rewarding the most
important over the best.

00:05:11.870 --> 00:05:15.360
It's an admirable effort,
but it has its limits.

00:05:15.360 --> 00:05:17.930
It ignores subjectivity entirely

00:05:17.930 --> 00:05:21.880
and promotes a criteria
that makes a film important.

00:05:21.880 --> 00:05:23.940
You might have heard
this criteria referred

00:05:23.940 --> 00:05:26.160
to as Oscar bait.

00:05:26.160 --> 00:05:28.750
An Oscar-bait film attempts to appeal

00:05:28.750 --> 00:05:31.260
to this specific formula in order to win,

00:05:31.260 --> 00:05:34.630
providing a safer
investment for the studios.

00:05:34.630 --> 00:05:38.410
These films are often adapted
from a famous source material,

00:05:38.410 --> 00:05:39.800
based on a true story;

00:05:39.800 --> 00:05:42.180
even better, they include period dramas

00:05:42.180 --> 00:05:45.210
with lavish costumes, and
historical biographies

00:05:45.210 --> 00:05:47.380
of important figures, bonus points

00:05:47.380 --> 00:05:49.500
for characters with a disability.

00:05:49.500 --> 00:05:54.160
Since 1980, 89% of the
best picture winners fit

00:05:54.160 --> 00:05:57.370
into at least one of
these five categories.

00:05:57.370 --> 00:06:02.250
These films also meet a
similar criteria: mass appeal.

00:06:02.250 --> 00:06:04.560
Oscar voters love critically and

00:06:04.560 --> 00:06:06.530
commercially successful movies.

00:06:06.530 --> 00:06:10.240
Of every best picture winner,
82% have a critic's rating

00:06:10.240 --> 00:06:15.240
above 80, and 78% have an
audience rating above 80 as well.

00:06:15.640 --> 00:06:17.640
This is a perfect representation

00:06:17.640 --> 00:06:20.840
of how the Academy votes for best picture.

00:06:20.840 --> 00:06:23.060
They use a preferential voting system

00:06:23.060 --> 00:06:25.560
where the voters are
asked to rank the nominees

00:06:25.560 --> 00:06:29.050
in order to reward collective preferences.

00:06:29.050 --> 00:06:32.660
This means that the
least disliked films will

00:06:32.660 --> 00:06:35.460
always win over smaller, niche movies

00:06:35.460 --> 00:06:37.990
with ardent fan followings.

00:06:37.990 --> 00:06:39.980
It also means that the Oscars will

00:06:39.980 --> 00:06:42.540
almost always go with a safer bet

00:06:42.540 --> 00:06:46.000
instead of films that try
for something different.

00:06:46.000 --> 00:06:50.060
This causes another problem: fatigue.

00:06:50.060 --> 00:06:52.430
Because the Oscars prefer certain movies

00:06:52.430 --> 00:06:54.850
over others, their nominations

00:06:54.850 --> 00:06:58.960
and winners look pretty
much the same every year,

00:06:58.960 --> 00:07:01.780
whether it's a film
about journalists trying

00:07:01.780 --> 00:07:04.690
to uncover the truth or a historical epic

00:07:04.690 --> 00:07:07.470
about a man against a nation,

00:07:07.470 --> 00:07:11.020
it's something we've all seen before.

00:07:11.020 --> 00:07:13.350
And the same goes for talent.

00:07:13.350 --> 00:07:18.350
Since 1970, about 71% of best
picture winners had a director

00:07:18.520 --> 00:07:22.330
or a cast member who was
previously nominated for an Oscar,

00:07:22.330 --> 00:07:25.000
a trend that carries until today.

00:07:25.000 --> 00:07:28.390
Sure, it feels great to
watch DiCaprio win an Oscar

00:07:28.390 --> 00:07:32.010
he well deserves, but
seeing the same people

00:07:32.010 --> 00:07:37.010
over and over and over
again every year signifies

00:07:37.010 --> 00:07:41.840
that other creative independent
voices are not being heard.

00:07:41.840 --> 00:07:44.600
The Oscars are dominated
by enormous studios.

00:07:44.600 --> 00:07:46.930
52% of best picture winners are

00:07:46.930 --> 00:07:50.020
from the six major Hollywood film studios,

00:07:50.020 --> 00:07:52.670
but that number rises to 81% if

00:07:52.670 --> 00:07:56.033
you include many major
film studios like MGM.

00:07:57.010 --> 00:08:00.330
The Oscars are dying, and they have no

00:08:00.330 --> 00:08:02.550
one to blame but themselves.

00:08:02.550 --> 00:08:05.030
What started out as a celebration

00:08:05.030 --> 00:08:08.240
of great achievements
in filmmaking has turned

00:08:08.240 --> 00:08:10.470
into nothing but a business strategy

00:08:10.470 --> 00:08:13.390
for studios who compete for profit.

00:08:13.390 --> 00:08:15.360
And the ones who suffer the most

00:08:15.360 --> 00:08:19.520
from it are none other
than you, the viewers,

00:08:19.520 --> 00:08:21.370
who no longer gain the insight

00:08:21.370 --> 00:08:24.120
or the entertainment that was promised.

00:08:24.120 --> 00:08:27.350
And all the drama, the issues,
and the snubs will continue

00:08:27.350 --> 00:08:29.730
on until the Oscars realize what

00:08:29.730 --> 00:08:32.260
their event is really all about:

00:08:32.260 --> 00:08:33.593
movies.

